## Walking a drive from a resolver

A custom subgraph that searches or aggregates across a drive needs the drive's contents. In v6 that is a relationship query on the reactor client, not a walk over a `nodes` array:

```ts
export const getResolvers = (subgraph: BaseSubgraph) => {
  const reactorClient = subgraph.reactorClient;

  return {
    Query: {
      searchTodos: async (
        parent: unknown,
        args: { driveId: string; searchTerm: string },
      ) => {
        const children = await reactorClient.getOutgoingRelationships(
          args.driveId,
          "child",
        );
        const todoItems: string[] = [];
        for (const doc of children.results) {
          if (doc.header.documentType !== "powerhouse/todo-list") continue;
          const todoDoc = doc as TodoListDocument;
          const hits = todoDoc.state.global.items.filter((e) =>
            e.text.includes(args.searchTerm),
          ).length;
          if (hits > 0) todoItems.push(doc.header.id);
        }
        return todoItems;
      },
    },
  };
};
```

Three details to carry over into your own resolvers:

- **`"child"` is the relationship type.** Drive membership is modelled as an outgoing relationship from the drive document, which is why the same call generalises to any relationship you define.
- **Results come back as a page**, read via `children.results` — not a bare array. Large drives require paging, so a resolver that assumes one page is correct only by accident.
- **Type filtering is yours to do.** The call returns documents of every type in the drive; each carries `header.documentType`, and skipping the ones you do not care about is resolver code, not a query parameter.

The same `getOutgoingRelationships` call backs the built-in reactor resolvers, so this is the supported path rather than a workaround.

_Source: apps/academy/docs/academy/03-Build/04-WorkWithData/03-UsingSubgraphs.md, packages/reactor-api/src/graphql/reactor/resolvers.ts (Powerhouse v6.2.2-dev.45)._
